From 5675e45b486925b7a56ab01d1b7ed6520d617d31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: IOS-POC-26-2b backport <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 02:33:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avformat/hls: anchor discontinuity sequences to the playlist
 timeline

Adapts FongMi/FFmpeg@5805f9364c2e9a5f6ce625c9077b308c3ed4014d for WebHTV
(IOS-POC-26-2b), on top of the n8.1.2 port of that commit.

5805f936 only corrects a timestamp jump larger than
max(2 * TARGETDURATION, 1 s) relative to the previous packet; smaller
backward jumps are clamped and smaller forward ones pass. Common ad layouts
jump by less: a pre-roll followed by content restarting near 0, a mid-roll
whose content clock skips the ad, back-to-back ads on their own clocks.
Linear playback then stayed 15-28 s off the playlist timeline, with
hundreds of pinned duplicate timestamps, and seeks disagreed with the shown
time.

For playlists that are finished when first normalized (EXT-X-ENDLIST):

- EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY is kept per segment. read_data_continuous() records
  the byte position at which each opened segment starts (only for finished
  playlists with discontinuities, with the EXTINF sums accumulated
  incrementally), and packets are attributed to their segment by pkt->pos,
  so late packets of an earlier segment (demuxer and parser delay) keep the
  offset their stream last used in that sequence instead of being taken for
  the new one. A video frame held across an audio-only ad therefore keeps
  the content's offset, not the ad's.
- The first packet delivered from a new discontinuity sequence decides the
  offset of that sequence, shared by the playlist's streams, which keeps
  the segment's own A/V relation. Mapped with the current offset, it keeps
  that offset only if it continues its stream within 1.5 frames and at
  most 60 ms (continuous across the tag), or continues it within 1 s while
  its playlist position is more than 1 s away (EXTINF drift, e.g. integer
  EXTINF). Otherwise it is re-anchored to its segment's playlist start:
  first_timestamp + the EXTINF durations before it + the stream baseline,
  the value seeking uses (RFC 8216 section 6.3.3, which is what AVPlayer
  and Media3's per-discontinuity TimestampAdjuster follow). Timestamps
  really continuous across the tag are left exactly as stock delivers
  them. A sequence further off its position than that window (1.5 frames,
  at most 60 ms) is re-anchored rather than kept, so it neither steps back
  nor passes its offset on to the content after it; one inside the window
  keeps its offset, which then stays in the content after it (+0.040 and
  +0.060 s for an ad 1 and 1.5 frames off at 25 fps, as in the port).
- No video dts steps back. A deciding video packet at or behind its last
  dts, and the first video packet of a sequence that audio decided, are
  moved to one frame after that dts, with the shared offset. Deciding audio
  may overlap its previous packet by less than a packet (its duration, or
  its dts step since MPEG-TS audio has none here), as on a continuous
  splice.
  Accepted consequence: when a sequence whose encode ends at the cut (an
  ad, or content before a post-roll) has audio leading its video by 2048
  AAC samples or more (encoder priming; FFmpeg's own encoder uses 1024),
  the first audio of the sequence after it overlaps that audio by two
  packets or more, so that sequence is delayed by the overlap, and the
  delay adds up across the following cuts until a seek re-anchors. A/V
  stay together and seeks land exactly. Measured with 2048- and
  2112-sample leads: +0.044-0.061 s per ad-to-ad cut, +0.044-0.093 s per
  ad-to-content cut, +0.059-0.064 s per content-to-post-roll cut;
  0.097-0.129 s after one two-ad break (up to 0.178 s when the break
  follows a seek), 0.232 s after two. Allowing an overlap of less than
  four packets removes most of it (0-0.096 s) but steps audio back by up
  to 0.031-0.040 s at ad exits.
  Accepted consequence: when the media before a splice runs longer than
  its EXTINF, what follows is delayed by that overrun, rounded up to a
  frame, and the delay persists, adding up across breaks, until a sequence
  has room to land on its position or a seek re-anchors. Measured: +0.060 s
  after one 60 ms understatement, +0.120 s after a video-only ad whose
  media overruns its last EXTINF by 120 ms; 2.4 s after 60 breaks whose
  content EXTINF is 40 ms short each (port 6.24 s, stock 596 s). The
  alternative, landing on the position, repeats a video dts and steps
  audio back by more than a frame at every such break.
  Likewise the first packet decides with its own baseline: when an ad's
  audio leads its video, the audio lands on the position and the video
  follows late by the lead (0.2 s lead: 0.200 s, port 0.168 s), as with a
  TimestampAdjuster initialised by the first sample.
  Accepted consequence: audio following a sequence that video decided
  keeps the shared offset, so when the new video has a shorter dts-to-pts
  reorder delay than the previous video, that audio steps back by the
  difference plus the source's own overlap (less than a packet): 0.024 s at
  a 25 to 30 fps splice, 0.064 s from 15 fps content into a 25 fps ad.
  Moving the offset on instead delays the video already delivered and the
  rest of the sequence (tried: 0.013 -> 0.045 s linear on the 30 fps
  splices, 0.053 -> 0.160 s at 15 fps).
- A stream's baseline is measured at its first packet against its own
  segment start on the mapped timeline. For a stream present from the start
  this is dts - first_timestamp as before; a stream first seen later (a
  video-only pre-roll followed by A/V content on another clock) no longer
  carries its distance to the playlist start into every re-anchor it
  decides. The measurement falls back to the playlist's own baseline when
  it is more than 1 s away from it, or more than 100 ms for a stream first
  seen after the playlist's first segment, which must then start at its
  segment start. Audio measures it again where it starts together with the
  video: at its first packet of a sequence that another stream decided or
  a seek anchored, in that packet's segment, and, while its baseline is
  more than 100 ms from the playlist's (a start delay inside the first
  segment), at its first packet of each later segment; a new value is only
  taken within 100 ms of the playlist's baseline. Audio starting 0.6 s
  after video inside the first segment therefore no longer places every
  cut it decides 0.6 s late (0.643 s -> 0.043 s with an ad before the
  cut, 0.077 s without one; port 0.080 and 0.040 s).
  Known limitation: a video stream first seen when it decides a sequence
  (an audio-only first segment whose PMT already lists video) takes the
  playlist's baseline, so its frames land late by its reorder delay:
  +0.080 s at 25 fps and +0.133 s at 15 fps with 2 B-frames (port and stock
  56 s off).
- After a seek, the anchor is taken from the seek stream's first packet, at
  the start of the segment that packet comes from (the seek segment, unless
  it has no packet of that stream). The other streams' packets read before
  it are discarded anyway and no longer touch any state. The landing
  keyframe then maps exactly onto the seek threshold, and post-seek
  time-pos matches linear playback.

Live playlists keep the continuity-only mapping of 5805f936 unchanged.

The seek discard compared the packet dts rounded down to microseconds with
the threshold, while normalized timestamps are rounded to the nearest
tick, so a segment's first keyframe could compare 1 us short and be
dropped, landing one GOP late (EXTINF 15.166667, 16.633333, 6.666667 ...).
For playlists whose timestamps are normalized, compare in the packet's time
base against the threshold rounded down (ff_hls_timestamp_reached());
playlists without discontinuities keep the upstream comparison. The
caa3fa6a/e27ad576 clamp and baseline semantics are unchanged.

Tested on 76 local fixture playlists (MPEG-TS and fMP4; VOD, event, live;
master with alternate audio; ads with 1024- to 2112-sample AAC priming)
against stock n8.1.2 and the port. Playlists without EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY,
or with timestamps continuous across it, are byte-identical to stock in
play and seeks, except a lone tag before the first segment, which plays
as the port (same timing, one audio packet differs); live and event
playlists are identical to the port. On VOD with discontinuities, video
dts strictly increase everywhere, no pinned span exceeds one frame, every
in-range seek lands on the right frame except where the playlist has no
video at the target, the late video above, a splice into an ad of a
higher frame rate (15 to 25 fps: time-pos -0.053 s, one target +0.060 s)
and EXTINF that misstates the media, and linear playback stays within
0.1 s of the EXTINF timeline on every ad layout except the accepted
consequences and the known limitation above and the port behaviour
below. As in the port (within 0.04 s): +/-1.5 s EXTINF drift (seeks
follow EXTINF, playback the media) and masters where only one rendition
carries the tags. Playlists without discontinuities keep stock's seeks,
including one GOP late when the audio leads the video by more than a
frame but less than its reorder delay (upstream, not addressed here).

No public API/ABI change: hls.c internals and the internal
hls_timestamp.{c,h} only. libavformat/tests/hls_timestamp.c gains cases for
continuity within tolerance, EXTINF drift, pre-roll reset, mid-roll content
skip, backward and forward jumps, late packets (including one held across a
sequence without its stream), both keep legs, sequences 80 ms and 0.8 s
before/after their position (and the content after them) and on a repeated
clock, half-frame jitter, the 60 ms continuity limit at 12.5 fps, the
monotonic moves (re-anchor, kept offset, a following video stream; audio
sub-frame overlap with and without packet durations), streams appearing
after the playlist start and mid-segment, a start delay inside the first
segment with and without an ad before the cut, both seek-anchor cases and
the rounding case.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
